      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a Gram-negative pathogenic bacterium responsible for severe infections, and it is naturally resistant to many clinically approved antibiotic families. Oxazolidinone antibiotics are active against many Gram-positive bacteria, but are inactive against P. aeruginosa. Increasing the uptake of oxazolidinones through the bacterial envelope could lead to an increased antibiotic effect. Pyochelin is a siderophore of P. aeruginosa which delivers external iron to the bacterial cytoplasm and is a potential vector for the development of Trojan Horse oxazolidinone conjugates. Novel pyochelin-oxazolidinone conjugates were synthesized using an unexpectedly regioselective peptide coupling between an amine functionalized pyochelin and oxazolidinones functionalized with a terminal carboxylate."
      },
